# Health checks

Load balancers, container platforms and uptime monitors ask an app, every
few seconds, whether it is still up. `howdy.health_check` gives them an
answer at `/up`:

```gleam
import howdy
import howdy/health

pub fn app() -> howdy.App {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(pages())
  |> howdy.health_check(health.new())
}
```

```sh
curl -i http://127.0.0.1:8787/up
```

```
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
cache-control: no-store

{"status":"ok"}
```

Projects made by [`howdy new`](/docs/cli/new) mount it already, last in
`app()`, and their `Dockerfile` asks it.

`GET` and `HEAD` are answered. Other methods, and other paths, go to the
app's routes as usual.

## Answered before everything else

The health check is answered before routing and before the app's
middleware. That keeps it working whatever the app does to other
requests:

- rate limits, guards, CSRF checks and redirects to HTTPS cannot turn the
  prober away
- the [request logger](/docs/middleware/logging) does not write a line for
  every probe
- no trace is recorded, so probes do not crowd out real requests in
  [telemetry](/docs/telemetry/overview)

A route of the app's own at the same path is never reached by `GET` or
`HEAD`. Use `health.at` to answer somewhere else:

```gleam
howdy.health_check(app, health.new() |> health.at("/healthz"))
```

## Checking what the app depends on

With no checks, a `200` means the app started and is accepting
connections. Add a check for anything the app cannot serve without:

```gleam
import howdy/database

health.new()
|> health.check("database", database.health_check(db))
```

`database.health_check` from [Howdy Database](/docs/database/overview) runs
`SELECT 1`. A check of your own is any function that returns `Ok(Nil)` when
all is well, or an `Error` with the reason it is not:

```gleam
health.new()
|> health.check("database", database.health_check(db))
|> health.check("search", fn() {
  case search.ping(client) {
    Ok(_) -> Ok(Nil)
    Error(e) -> Error(search.describe(e))
  }
})
```

The checks run at the same time. If every one passes the answer is `200`;
if any fails, crashes or runs out of time it is `503`. Either way the
response names each check and how it went:

```
HTTP/1.1 503 Service Unavailable

{"status":"error","checks":{"database":"error","search":"ok"}}
```

A check's status is `ok`, `error` or `timeout`. The reason a check failed
is never sent, as anyone can ask for the page: it is logged instead, at
most once every ten seconds for each check.

## Timeouts

The checks get one second between them by default. A check still running
then is stopped and reported as `timeout`. Change it with `health.timeout`:

```gleam
health.new()
|> health.check("database", database.health_check(db))
|> health.timeout(3000)
```

Keep it below the prober's own timeout, or the prober gives up before the
answer arrives. A Kubernetes probe waits one second by default.

## What to check

A failing health check usually takes the app out of service, and some
platforms restart it. So check what a restart or a different instance
could fix, and keep the checks cheap:

- An app that connects to its database and runs its migrations in
  `main`, as `howdy new` projects do, refuses to start without them. A
  deploy that cannot work then never reports healthy, even with no checks
  at all.
- A check of a shared dependency fails on every instance at once. Taking
  them all out of service will not bring the database back, and
  restarting them all at once makes the recovery slower.

Platforms that ask two questions get two paths. Call
`howdy.health_check` once for each:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(pages())
// Is the process alive? Restart it if not.
|> howdy.health_check(health.new() |> health.at("/up"))
// Can it serve traffic right now? Stop sending requests if not.
|> howdy.health_check(
  health.new()
  |> health.at("/ready")
  |> health.check("database", database.health_check(db)),
)
```

In Kubernetes, point the `livenessProbe` at `/up` and the
`readinessProbe` at `/ready`. Docker, Kamal and most load balancers ask
one question; give them `/up`.

## Probing from a container

A `HEALTHCHECK` in a Dockerfile runs inside the container, so it needs a
client there. Alpine images have `wget`:

```dockerfile
HEALTHCHECK --interval=10s --timeout=3s \
  CMD wget -qO /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:8787/up || exit 1
```

Probes from outside the container, such as a load balancer's, reach the
app only if it listens on every interface rather than `127.0.0.1`: bind it
to `0.0.0.0` in a container.

## Testing

`howdy/testing` sends requests through the health check like any other:

```gleam
pub fn health_check_test() {
  let response = testing.get("/up") |> testing.send(app())
  assert response.status == 200
}
```
